---
import Base from "../../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import DistaNav from "../../../../components/DistaNav.astro";
import DistaFooter from "../../../../components/DistaFooter.astro";
import { DISTA_REPO } from "../../../../data/dista";

const description =
  "How to use dista: tasks and actors, passing results between them, shuffles, checkpoints, running locally and on Kubernetes, and watching a cluster.";

const env = [
  ["DISTA_ROLE", "head or worker; unset, the process is a driver", "driver"],
  ["DISTA_HEAD", "host:port of the head, for a driver or a worker in a cluster", "local mode"],
  ["DISTA_PORT", "the port a head listens on", "6390"],
  ["DISTA_CONNECT_TIMEOUT", "seconds a driver or worker keeps retrying the head", "60"],
  ["DISTA_ADVERTISE", "the address a worker gives peers to fetch its results from", "its outgoing IP"],
  ["DISTA_STORE_DIR", "where a worker writes large results for its host to map", "/dev/shm, else $TMPDIR"],
  ["DISTA_SHM", "0 keeps large results in the worker's memory instead", "on"],
  ["DISTA_CHECKPOINT_URI", "where actor checkpoints go: a directory, file://, or s3://bucket/prefix", "none: checkpoints fail"],
  ["DISTA_METRICS_PORT", "the port a head or worker serves Prometheus metrics on, at /metrics", "off"],
  ["DISTA_DEBUG", "1 logs each task's inputs as a worker assembles them", "off"],
];
---

<Base title="Docs · dista" description={description} bare chrome={false}>
  <DistaNav />

  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide docs">
      <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Using dista</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        dista turns ordinary Mojo functions into tasks that run across a cluster. This page
        covers what a program needs: declaring tasks and actors, passing results around,
        and running the same binary locally and on Kubernetes. The design itself is in the
        repository's <a href={`${DISTA_REPO}/blob/main/DESIGN.md`} rel="noopener">DESIGN.md</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#install">Install</a>
        <a href="#tasks">Tasks</a>
        <a href="#results">Results</a>
        <a href="#graphs">Tasks on results</a>
        <a href="#shuffles">Shuffles</a>
        <a href="#types">What crosses the wire</a>
        <a href="#actors">Actors</a>
        <a href="#checkpoints">Checkpoints</a>
        <a href="#running">Running it</a>
        <a href="#watching">Watching a cluster</a>
        <a href="#timeline">The timeline</a>
        <a href="#failures">When things fail</a>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <p>
        dista isn't published as a package yet. A pixi project depends on it from source, at
        a pinned commit, since a git dependency without a revision never updates:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`[dependencies]
dista-mojo = { git = "${DISTA_REPO}", rev = "<commit>" }`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        It targets Mojo 1.2 and tracks the nightly toolchain. Reading Parquet and Iceberg
        tables uses magmalake's <code>parquet-mojo</code> and <code>iceberg-mojo</code>
        alongside it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tasks">Tasks</h2>
      <p>
        A task is a top-level <code>def</code> taking one argument, declared once with its
        argument and result types, and registered in the runtime's parameter list:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`from dista import Runtime, Task

def square(x: Int) -> Int:
    return x * x

def describe(x: Int) raises -> String:
    if x < 0:
        raise Error("negative")
    return String("x = ", x)

comptime Square = Task[Int, Int, square]
comptime Describe = Task[Int, String, describe]

def main() raises:
    var rt = Runtime[Square, Describe](workers=4)
    var r = rt.remote[Square](7)
    print(rt.get(r))  # 49`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>rt.remote</code> returns at once with an <code>ObjectRef</code>;
        <code>rt.get</code> waits for the result, or raises the task's error.
        <code>rt.ready(r)</code> asks without waiting. Every process in a cluster runs the
        same binary, so tasks are named on the wire by a hash of the function, and a process
        built with different tasks is refused when it connects.
      </p>

      <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
      <p>
        A small result (up to 64 KB) comes back to the driver through the head. A larger one
        stays on the worker that computed it: in a shared-memory file on that host, which a
        reader on the same host maps without copying, or in the worker's memory, which a
        reader elsewhere fetches from that worker directly. <code>rt.location(r)</code> says
        where a result lives.
      </p>
      <p>
        The head counts references to every result: the driver's own, until
        <code>rt.release(r)</code>, and one for every pending task that takes it as input.
        At zero the result is deleted wherever it is. <code>rt.stats()</code> reports what
        the head holds.
      </p>

      <h2 id="graphs">Tasks on results</h2>
      <p>
        A task can take another task's result instead of a value. The head runs it once the
        input exists, and the worker running it fetches the input from wherever it lives. A
        list of refs is gathered, in order, into a <code>Seq</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`from dista import Seq

def total(xs: Seq[Int]) -> Int:
    var t = 0
    for i in range(len(xs)):
        t += xs[i]
    return t

comptime Total = Task[Seq[Int], Int, total]

var squares = List[ObjectRef[Int]]()
for i in range(100):
    squares.append(rt.remote[Square](i))
var sum = rt.remote[Total](squares)   # runs once all 100 exist
var next = rt.remote[Square](sum)     # a task on a task's result`}</code></pre>
      <p>If an input fails, every task that depends on it fails with that error.</p>

      <h2 id="shuffles">Shuffles</h2>
      <p>
        A shuffle moves data between two sets of tasks: map tasks each split their output
        by key, and each reduce task takes its share from every map task. Two kinds of task
        make that work. A <code>TaskMany</code> returns several results, each an object of
        its own, stored, fetched and freed separately, like Ray's <code>num_returns</code>.
        A <code>TaskWith</code> takes a value beside a list of other tasks' results:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`from dista import TaskMany, TaskWith

def map_rows(m: Int) -> Seq[Seq[Pair]]:          # one partition per reducer
    ...
def reduce_part(j: Int, parts: Seq[Seq[Pair]]) -> Seq[Pair]:
    ...

comptime MapRows = TaskMany[Int, Seq[Pair], map_rows]
comptime ReducePart = TaskWith[Int, Seq[Pair], Seq[Pair], reduce_part]

var outputs = List[List[ObjectRef[Seq[Pair]]]]()
for m in range(8):
    outputs.append(rt.remote_many[MapRows](m, 4))   # 4 results each
for j in range(4):
    var mine = List[ObjectRef[Seq[Pair]]]()
    for m in range(8):
        mine.append(outputs[m][j])
    var r = rt.remote[ReducePart](j, mine)          # partition j of every map`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        A <code>TaskMany</code> must return exactly as many values as asked for, or every
        one of its results fails. Each large partition stays on the worker that made it,
        and the reducer fetches it from there.
      </p>

      <h2 id="types">What crosses the wire</h2>
      <p>
        Arguments and results are encoded by type, at compile time. Scalars,
        <code>Bool</code> and <code>String</code> have fixed encodings. Any struct is
        encoded field by field. Arrow <code>RecordBatch</code>es travel as Arrow IPC, so
        columnar data keeps its format between tasks. A type that needs its own encoding
        implements the <code>Codec</code> trait. A bare <code>List</code> doesn't encode:
        send <code>Seq</code>, a struct, or Arrow instead.
      </p>

      <h2 id="actors">Actors</h2>
      <p>
        An actor is state that lives on one worker between calls: a type, the function that
        builds it, and methods that take it <code>mut</code>. Each actor gets a worker of its
        own, and its calls run one at a time, in the order they were made. A method's
        result is an <code>ObjectRef</code> like a task's, so a task can take it as input.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`from dista import Actor, Method

@fieldwise_init
struct Counter(Movable):
    var total: Int

def new_counter(start: Int) -> Counter:
    return Counter(start)

def add(mut c: Counter, n: Int) -> Int:
    c.total += n
    return c.total

comptime CounterActor = Actor[Counter, Int, new_counter]
comptime Add = Method[CounterActor, Int, Int, add]

var rt = Runtime[CounterActor, Add, Square](workers=2)
var c = rt.create[CounterActor](0)
var n = rt.call[Add](c, 5)            # ObjectRef[Int]
print(rt.get(rt.remote[Square](n)))   # 25
rt.kill(c)                             # after its earlier calls; frees the worker`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Calling a method on the wrong actor's handle is a compile error. A handle is a
        value, so a task or another actor can call the actor too: declare it with
        <code>CtxTask</code> or <code>CtxMethod</code>, and its function gets a
        <code>Client</code> to call through:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`from dista import Client, CtxTask

def bump(mut c: Client, h: ActorHandle[CounterActor]) raises -> Int:
    return c.get(c.call[Add](h, 1))

comptime Bump = CtxTask[ActorHandle[CounterActor], Int, bump]
var n = rt.remote[Bump](c)   # a task calling the actor`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>When an actor's worker goes away</strong>, the head places the actor on
        another worker, builds it again from its last checkpoint, or its constructor if it
        has none, and runs every call since then again, in order. Each call is answered
        once. A call may run twice on the actor's state, so a method should be safe to
        repeat; that's the price of not losing calls.
      </p>

      <h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
      <p>
        A checkpoint is the actor's state through the codec, written to
        <code>DISTA_CHECKPOINT_URI</code>: a directory every node sees, or
        <code>s3://bucket/prefix</code>. Ask for one every so many calls, or when it
        suits:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`var c = rt.create[CounterActor](0, checkpoint_every=100)
...
var calls = rt.get(rt.checkpoint(c))   # written; covers this many calls`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        A restart then replays only the calls since the checkpoint, and the head stops
        keeping the ones it covers. A state type that implements <code>Codec</code> decides
        what it saves: a cache can save nothing, a loaded model its path. Checkpoints also
        carry an actor across a head restart: the new head restores it from its newest
        checkpoint, and its callers send again the calls no checkpoint includes. Killing an
        actor deletes its checkpoints.
      </p>

      <h2 id="running">Running it</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Locally,</strong> <code>Runtime(workers=n)</code> forks a head and
        <code>n</code> workers and drives them from the same process, which is how tests
        and laptops run. <strong>On a cluster,</strong> the same binary takes its role from
        the environment: one head, any number of workers, and drivers that connect to the
        head.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {env.map(([k, v, d]) => (
              <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td><td class="ref">{d}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The head doesn't run your code, so it can be the generic one: <code>dista
        head</code>, from the <code>dista</code> command (<code>pixi run cli</code>). It
        serves drivers and workers built from any program, several at once, and gives each
        driver's tasks and actors only to workers built from the same program. A head built
        into your program, <code>DISTA_ROLE=head</code>, serves only that program.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repository's <code>deploy/</code> folder builds one image for every role, with
        your program at <code>/opt/dista/bin/app</code> and the command at
        <code>/opt/dista/bin/dista</code>; the manifests run the head as <code>dista
        head</code>. <code>tests/kind.sh</code> runs a cluster on kind, and
        <code>bench/cloud/</code> runs one on EC2 with k3s. Give the workers'
        <code>/dev/shm</code> room for the large results they keep. A deleted pod's process
        exits on its SIGTERM.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>Runtime(pipeline=n)</code> sets how many tasks the head keeps in flight per
        worker (default 2).
      </p>

      <h2 id="watching">Watching a cluster</h2>
      <p>
        <code>dista status</code> prints the head's view: workers, with the program each
        was built from and what it's running or hosting; drivers; and actors, with their
        worker, calls and last checkpoint. <code>dista timeline -o trace.json</code> writes
        every driver's finished tasks, and <code>dista stop</code> shuts the head and its
        workers down. Each takes <code>--head host:port</code>, or reads
        <code>DISTA_HEAD</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <code>DISTA_METRICS_PORT</code> set, the head and every worker serve
        Prometheus metrics at <code>/metrics</code>: tasks submitted and finished by
        outcome, task durations as a histogram, queue depth, objects and bytes held,
        workers, drivers, actors, restarts and checkpoints. The kind manifests turn it on
        at port 9090, for Kubernetes monitoring to scrape.
      </p>

      <h2 id="timeline">The timeline</h2>
      <p>
        The head stamps each task's submission, dispatch and completion, and the worker its
        start and end. <code>rt.timeline(path)</code> writes every finished task, in
        Chrome's trace format, which <a href="https://ui.perfetto.dev" rel="noopener">Perfetto</a>
        and the browser's trace viewer open. Each worker is a track, with a second track for
        time spent waiting for inputs. The driver can add its own work, so a gap between
        tasks says what it was:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`var t = rt.clock()
var tasks = scan.plan_files()
rt.span("plan", t)          # from t to now, on the driver's track
...
_ = rt.timeline("trace.json")`}</code></pre>

      <h2 id="failures">When things fail</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>A task that raises</strong> fails its <code>ObjectRef</code> with the error,
        and the tasks that depend on it. <strong>A worker that dies</strong> fails the tasks
        it was running and loses the results it held. <strong>A head that dies</strong> is
        rebuilt from its peers when it comes back: workers rejoin and report what they hold,
        and drivers reconnect under their old number and resubmit what they still need. A
        task running at that moment may run twice, so tasks should be safe to repeat, as a
        pure function is. Actors come back too, from their checkpoints (see above). A chaos
        test kills the head at random throughout a run and checks every answer, and on kind
        the actor's pod is deleted mid-run and its count must come out exact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <DistaFooter />
